  3. SwiftUI Rules

Use @State for local, view-only state.

Use @StateObject for owning a reference to a ViewModel.

Use @ObservedObject when receiving a ViewModel from elsewhere.

Use @EnvironmentObject sparingly for global shared state.

Always extract preview providers for UI testing.

Use modifiers in logical order:

Layout (frame, padding)

Styling (background, foregroundColor)

Effects (shadow, animation)

4. Concurrency & Data

Use Swift Concurrency (async/await) instead of completion handlers where possible.

Always mark UI-updating calls with @MainActor.

Use Task {} carefully inside views — prefer delegating async work to view models.

Wrap networking in a NetworkService with URLSession + Codable.

5. Error Handling

Use Result<T, Error> or throws for async calls.

Provide meaningful error messages, not just .localizedDescription.

Avoid silent failures — log errors with os_log or a logging utility.

6. Testing

Write unit tests for:

ViewModels (business logic)

Services (network, persistence)

Use SwiftUI Previews and UI Tests for Views.

7. Cursor AI Usage Rules

Always follow the MVVM project structure.

Never mix business logic inside SwiftUI Views. Put it in ViewModels.

Always prefer async/await over completion closures unless interoperability requires otherwise.

Do not generate storyboards or UIKit code unless explicitly asked.

All new SwiftUI Views must include a preview.

Write concise, readable code over clever/obscure code.